stantial policy proposals to better  protect those living in long-term care  our chief political correspondent david  aiken takes a closer look  the pandemic data has been crystal clear  the vast majority of coven 19 deaths in  canada were in long-term care homes and  those in long-term care homes operated  for profit were sick and died at a much  higher rate than those in non-profit  homes you know the research is quite  clear overwhelmingly not-for-profit has  provided a better quality of care than  for-profit homes that's why the ndp is  pushing a ban on the practice of running  long-term care homes for profit we've  said really clearly it is wrong that  for-profit exists in this system that  there is there should be no profit that  puts people  as a less priority than than profits and  money maureen mcdermott's 91 year old  mother died of covid in a for-profit  long-term care facility the stories are  horrendous  profits do not belong inside of  long-term care and in fact  profits kill  seniors  singh and mcdermott push for the ban  while standing outside of the corporate  headquarters of rivera that's a private  for-profit ltc operator controlled  coincidentally by the federal public  sector pension investment board a crown  corporation  out of all private for-profit long-term  care operators they actually have the  highest number of deaths in canada over  860 deaths across 56 facilities neither  the liberals nor the conservatives agree  with a ban on for-profit ltc facilities  but each party has made substantial  commitments on long-term care the  conservatives would set aside three  billion dollars for upgrades and repairs  to long-term care homes and have  promised to quote encourage non-profit  operators the liberals are promising to  spend nine billion dollars on long-term  care establish a minimum wage of 25  dollars an hour for personal support  workers and hire 50 000 more of them  and so for the first time in a federal  election taking care of people i